it's lights out and away we go in a  world struggling with a climate  emergency the gas-guzzling cars of  Formula One have an image problem the  sport produces the equivalent to a  quarter of a million tons of carbon  dioxide on and off the track every  season but in an exclusive interview  with Sky News the f1 boss committed to  reducing carbon emissions to Net Zero by  2030 we think we can play a real  leadership role in terms of reducing  carbon emissions and ultimately  eliminating carbon emissions from the  combustion engine and I think that is  through both fuel technologies biofuels  potentially down the road things like  hydrogen fuels as well as energy  recapture there's another risk that in  effect you're supporting a redundant  technology that the internal combustion  engine the traditional petrol and diesel  car come 2030 will be phased out I think  the numbers something like you know a  billion cars are combustion engine cars  and to the degree we can be a platform  that shows what's possible in reducing  emissions and creating you know more  efficiency through those cheap  combustion engines we think is an  incredibly important part of the story  and we think is an incredibly important  part of the solution  eliminating emissions from the entire  sport is a huge challenge  less than 1% actually come from the  racecars themselves around 27% are  generated by business travel and hotels  and 26% by factories and operations but  by far the biggest proportion comes from  logistics 45% of the sports carbon  footprint comes from transporting cars  and equipment around the world  but it's the cars that grab the  headlines an environmental activists say  f1 should be far more ambitious I think  it's a real shame that they're not  putting the best frames that they have  into the genuinely new technologies that  we need in terms of electric vehicles  and for me that's a missed opportunity  I'm sure there are a lot of people  within f1 who would like to be going  